## Proof

**Claim.** In any normed vector space $(E, N)$ over $\mathbb{K}$ (with $\mathbb{K}=\mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{C}$), possibly infinite-dimensional,
$$\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)=\{0\}.$$

**Proof.** The inclusion $\{0\}\subseteq\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)$ is immediate, since every linear functional vanishes at $0$.

For the reverse inclusion, let $x\in E$ with $x\neq 0$, so that $N(x)>0$. We must exhibit some $f\in E'$ with $f(x)\neq 0$.

Consider the one-dimensional subspace $M=\mathbb{K}x\subseteq E$. Define a linear functional $g:M\to\mathbb{K}$ by
$$g(\lambda x)=\lambda\, N(x),\qquad \lambda\in\mathbb{K}.$$
This is well defined since $x\neq 0$ makes $\{x\}$ a basis of $M$. Moreover $g$ is continuous on $M$ (every linear functional on a finite-dimensional normed space is continuous), and for every $\lambda\in\mathbb{K}$,
$$|g(\lambda x)|=|\lambda|\,N(x)=N(\lambda x),$$
so $g$ has operator norm $1$ on $M$ and is dominated by the norm: $|g(y)|\le N(y)$ for all $y\in M$.

By the **Hahn–Banach (dominated extension) theorem**, $g$ extends to a linear functional $f:E\to\mathbb{K}$ satisfying
$$|f(y)|\le N(y)\quad\text{for all }y\in E.$$
This inequality shows that $f$ is continuous (it is $1$-Lipschitz), hence $f\in E'$. Evaluating at $x$:
$$f(x)=g(x)=N(x)\neq 0.$$
Therefore $x\notin\operatorname{Ker}(f)$, so $x\notin\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)$.

Since $x\neq 0$ was arbitrary,
$$\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)\subseteq\{0\}.$$

Combining both inclusions yields $\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)=\{0\}$. $\blacksquare$

**Remark on the role of Hahn–Banach.** The conclusion can fail for *algebraic* duals in the absence of choice-like extension principles, but for the *topological* dual $E'$ of a normed space it holds unconditionally thanks to Hahn–Banach, with no finite-dimensionality assumption. The key point is that a single nonzero vector always supports a continuous linear functional separating it from $0$.

